{{c|{{smaller|''A'', southwest tower, Cathedral of Bayeux, Normandy; ''B'', Church of St. Nicholas, Walcot, England.}}}}
and by some writers on architecture, a spire which rises directly from the walls of its tower, without parapets and gutters. —'''11.''' A narrow-pointed chisel used by masons for hewing stones. —'''12.''' Any tapered boring-bit or drill. {{smaller|Broaches used for boring wood are fluted like the shell-bit, but tapered toward the point; but those used in boring metal are solid, and usually three-, four-, or six-sided. Their common forms are shown in the annexed figures. Broaches are also known as ''wideners'' and ''reamers''.}}

